CHAPTER VII
FUNDING
Article 15
(1) Funding for the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence includes:
a. medical services, which include doctor examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, and medication costs;
b. medicolegal services, which include examinations for Visum et Repertum and Visum et Psikiatrikum;
c. psychosocial services, which consist of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelters);
d. legal services;
e. economic empowerment, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access.
(2) Funding management is carried out through technical institutions appointed by the Regional Government which is conducted transparently and accounted for in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
(3) Sources of funds in the implementation of protection for women and children are sourced from the State Revenue and Expenditure Budget, the Provincial Revenue and Expenditure Budget, the Sintang Regency APBD as well as other legitimate and non-binding sources.

Article 9
The obligations and responsibilities of the community are as follows:
a. making efforts to provide support for the development of violence prevention programs;
b. preparing proposals regarding the formulation and policies on protection;
c. making...
c. making efforts to provide protection and moral or material support to victims;
d. conducting supervision and reporting of acts of violence to the competent authorities.

Article 12
Forms of services for victims include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medicolegal services, which are a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. services...
c. psychosocial services, which are services provided by companions in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and provide social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength, and independence in resolving their problems;
d. Legal services, which are services to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process;
e. Economic independence services, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent;
f. Information Services (Informed Consent), related to services and assistance.

CHAPTER III
RIGHTS AND OBLIGATIONS OF VICTIMS
Article 5
Every victim has the right to:
a. obtain protection from the community and government as well as non-governmental institutions at the local, national and international levels;
b. obtain information about the location of complaint centers.
c. obtain integrated services in accordance with service principles;
d. file claims and/or legal suits through judicial bodies and/or courts outside the judiciary;
e. obtain information about laws and regulations that protect victims;
f. obtain confidential handling;
g. obtain...
g. obtain information and be involved in every decision-making process related to assistance and developments in case handling;
h. obtain guarantees of their rights related to their status as a wife, mother or child, family member, household member, and community member.
i. obtain assistance at every level of examination and throughout the judicial process.
j. obtain continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.
k. obtain protection from the disclosure of identity through mass media.

CHAPTER VII
FUNDING
Article 15
(1) Funding for the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence includes:
a. medical services, which include doctor examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, and medication costs;
b. medicolegal services, which include examinations for Visum et Repertum and Visum et Psikiatrikum;
c. psychosocial services, which consist of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelters);
